  "slug": "set-mismatch",
  "title": "Set Mismatch",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Cyclic sort",
  "patterns": [
    "cyclic sort",
    "array"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "if every value belongs at index value-1, what does it mean when you find an index i where nums[i] - 1 != i?",
    "try cyclic placement: repeatedly swap nums[i] to its home index v-1 unless that home index already holds the value v (which is your signal of a duplicate)",
    "once placement finishes, scan for the one index i where nums[i] != i+1 - the value sitting there is the duplicate, and i+1 is the missing number",
    "be careful not to swap a value with itself infinitely when it's a duplicate that already occupies a slot with the same value"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "returning [missing, duplicate] instead of [duplicate, missing] (order matters)",
    "infinite loop when the duplicate value and the value already at its home index are equal, since a naive swap condition never terminates",
    "confusing value and index (value v belongs at index v-1, not v)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array of length `n` that was supposed to contain every\nnumber from 1 to n exactly once. Due to an error, one number got\nduplicated, and as a result a different number from that range is now\nmissing entirely.\n\n**Return an array `[duplicate, missing]`.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "2 <= nums.length <= 10^4",
      "1 <= nums[i] <= nums.length",
      "exactly one value in [1, nums.length] occurs twice and exactly one occurs zero times"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 2 with the smallest possible array",
      "the duplicate and missing numbers are adjacent values",
      "the duplicate is at the very first or very last index"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
